AU  - Sassi, Najla
TI  - Enhancing Semantic Inference in Healthcare Systems With Ontology-Enriched NLP
T2  - IEEE ACCESS
M3  - Article
AB  - This study presents a novel semantic inference framework integrating advanced NLP techniques and domain-specific medical ontologies to improve extraction, interpretation, and utilization of clinical information from EHRs. The framework, tailored to oncology, combines transformer-based models such as BERT and custom-developed Named Entity Recognition algorithms with ontologies like SNOMED CT, UMLS, and cancer staging taxonomies. Evaluated on 50,000 anonymized oncology records, the system achieved a Named Entity Recognition precision of 94.2%, recall of 92.8%, and an F1-score of 93.5%, with inference accuracy reaching 88.3% and clinically relevant insights at 92.1%. The enhanced semantic search capabilities resulted in 87.3% MAP and 91.2% NDCG scores. Oncology experts reported 92% satisfaction with information access and 88% approval of inference suggestions, alongside an average 35% reduction in record review time. Our results indicate that the framework improves decision-making and workflow efficiency; external validation will determine its generalizability to broader healthcare applications.
