TI  - Labeling Function Generation for NER Task: Limitation of LLMs
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF 13TH ACM IKDD INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON DATA SCIENCE, CODS 2025
M3  - Proceedings Paper
CP  - 13th Conference on Data Sciences-CODS
CL  - Indian Institute of Science Education & Research (IISER) Pune, Pune, INDIA
AB  - Labeling Functions (LFs) are an important component in Programmatic Weak Supervision (PWS) pipeline introducing scalability for annotation tasks. LFs are built by the subject matter expert by looking at the dataset. Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently proven effective in generating LFs to support weak supervision across tasks such as review classification, topic classification, and sentiment analysis. These successes have largely been demonstrated in text classification settings, where rules can leverage surface-level cues like keywords or sentiment indicators. However, the use of LLMs to generate LFs for token classification tasks-particularly Named Entity Recognition (NER) - remains significantly understudied. This paper presents one of the first empirical and analytical investigations into whether LLMs can generate generalizable LFs for NER. While LLMs are capable of annotating entity spans when prompted directly, our experiments reveal that they consistently struggle to produce labeling logic that is semantically generalized. Through prompted LFs for NER we got highest F1-score of 16.66 in Gemini-2.0-flash through few-shot setting and the lowest F1-score of 0 in LLama 2 Chat 70b in zero-shot and few-shot setting. Through an evaluation framework, we show that the generated LFs tend to have high coverage but low abstraction, exhibiting fragile performance on unseen data. The results show that current LLMs are not good at generating semantically rich logic for token classification tasks like NER. This highlights the need for deeper research into how interpretable and robust the rules generated by LLMs really are.
