TI  - A self-feedback zero-shot information extraction framework via multi-round chain of thought
T2  - ENGINEERING APPLICATIONS OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE
M3  - Article
AB  - Zero-shot Information Extraction (ZIE) can extract key information without domain-specific labeled data. Large Language Model (LLM) is capable of efficiently extracting information from unseen domains, which greatly improves the generalization capability of information extraction and thus holds significant value. However, LLMbased information extraction approaches strongly rely on static predefined schemas, and predefining schemas in single-step extraction causes the LLM to discard entities and relationships outside the predefined schemas, thereby leading to information omission. Therefore, a multi-round Chain of Thought (CoT) with Zero-shot Information Extraction (MCOT-ZIE) is proposed. The MCOT-ZIE allows the LLM to freely extract entities and relationships in the initial stage of extraction without relying on predefined schemas, and elaborates the intermediate inference process to generate preliminary structured extraction results. Then, under the constraints of predefined entity and relationship patterns, the LLM summarizes the candidate types through mapping, mapping entities and relationships that do not meet the constraints to the predefined entity and relationship types, ensuring that the final extraction results are both flexible and meet strict standardization requirements. Finally, in the stages of information extraction and category mapping, a self-feedback chain is introduced, enabling the LLM to identify and correct omissions and errors in the preliminary information extraction and category mapping results. The MCOT-ZIE effectively solves the problem that information extraction approaches rely on static predefined schemas through free extraction and category mapping, and further reviews the results of information extraction and category mapping through the self-feedback chain, improving the accuracy of information extraction. In both Named Entity Recognition (NER) and Relation Extraction (RE) tasks, the proposed method achieves the best F1-score, significantly outperforming other supervised learning methods in fewshot scenarios and the prompt-based methods, demonstrating the advancement of the proposed method.
